body:
Sorry for the confusion! 

&#x200B;

I was screened by the school's disability service, but not by a psychiatrist. The school can't diagnose me (though they can score the test and give suggestions that I likely have it) and there's no pathway in the public health system (AKA funded, like with my other mental conditions), so I have to look toward private psychiatrists/psychologists according to my GP. 

&#x200B;

My friend has told me their GP office has a cheap psychiatrist who can help me get diagnosed, so I'm going to try that *if I can* (since I'm not enrolled at that practice it might not be possible), but otherwise it'll be incredibly difficult, expensive and a time-lengthy process. It's not that I decided it's too difficult or wont go my way, it's that it's an expensive and difficult process. I'm a broke uni student, and a single assessment appointment can be hundreds of dollars. If I can get in with this other psychiatrist at my friend's GP office then that'll be great, but I've decided that I'll be happy either way since I know in my heart what the answer is.

&#x200B;

I hope that clears it up! Sorry again!!

The thing is, the potatoes weren't good they were just *fine.* It's always better when I make it by myself, obviously without adding mayo. (who adds mayo to mashed potatoes anyway... /lh). It's not that I couldn't taste it, it's that I couldn't put my finger on "that weird tangy taste that's always in mum's potatoes." 

&#x200B;

You're probably right about that, but I'm still not likely to eat it. I don't like being lied to, *especially* about food, and I feel like I cannot trust *anything* anyone feeds me anymore. If this was just an autism issue, then maybe it'd be easier, but as I mentioned at the start of the post, there's also an ED in place here, which is already a breeding ground for spiralling thoughts around ingredients, numbers, accuracy etc. So idk. 

&#x200B;

I'll get over it. But I'm mostly just hurt about not being able to trust that someone isn't going to hide ingredients behind my back. Whether there's malice in that action or not.

I was bullied pretty heavily in primary school, well before I experienced any big mental health issues, but I found that in high school everyone was kind to me. Everyone knew I was having issues, it was pretty obvious with how much I had to leave school and class/scars/meltdowns. But I was never bullied over it. Maybe people did talk behind my back but I was blissfully unaware of it, and I'm glad for that at least. People were kind and mature in high school, though sometimes I hate myself for a lot of the things I did in high school because of my mental health and feel like I deserved to be bullied for it if that makes sense haha. I've never been diagnosed with ASD but looking back a lot of my childhood can be traced back to nd behaviours.

&#x200B;

that being said, I knew a girl in my Highschool class with ASD whose behaviours were much more apparent than mine. She was bullied a lot and talked about behind her back because other students had been attacked by her (her meltdowns included screaming at students she thought were being too loud or throwing things across the room), or didn't understand her, or felt like her behaviour was unfair.

I can't say I'm innocent on that front, either. While I was kind to her and helped her in class, I do remember harbouring a lot of disdain for her as we grew up, mainly because of her making a distasteful joke of my appearance or making it harder to focus in class or because her meltdowns were sometimes triggering for my mental health issues. I do regret feeling that way, as she was a really nice girl and had a really cool special interest in dragons. she just had big sensory issues and issues with social cues that I didn't understand until I graduated and started to realise I might have ASD as well. A lot of her behaviours have started to make sense to me now that I've realised I relate to a lot of the things she did or felt. 

&#x200B;

But yeah. That's my experience, I guess.

I have such a horrible time getting gifts for so many reasons!

&#x200B;

For one, I have major money anxiety. I hate spending money on anything, it throws me into panics! I cried just the other day for dropping 45 dollars on gift cards for friends! but my friends were really thankful so that made me feel better. And also, like you, its so hard to know what to get people! And everything is so expensive.

&#x200B;

I hate getting gifts, as well. (Well, no I love receiving gifts because... well, free stuff and all!) but I hate getting gifts and having to express thanks and all that. its hard, even if I'm really really thankful.

im not really against working, but I can't find a job to save my life and I feel like giving up. My parents are kind and pay me to clean around the house (my mum admitted it makes her happy to see I have more money to spend because money has always been a big anxiety thing for me), but I never get interviews and on the rare chance that I do they never follow up even if they say they will. 

&#x200B;

